Below is a step‐by‐step decomposition of the mission description into a goal model for multi‐robot systems. In this design, we identify a top‐level goal (G1) that can be achieved either via a sequential single‐robot process (collection then delivery) or, when multiple storages are involved, via parallel multi‐robot coordination. Each goal is decomposed into sub‐goals (and further into tasks) with clear runtime annotations (sequential “;”, parallel “#”, or fallback “FALLBACK(…)”) and with justifications based on the mission description.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Identify the Main Mission Goal  
─────────────────────────────  
The overall mission is to collect the required resources from designated storages and deliver them to a specified location. This leads to a top‐level goal (G1) with a target condition that all resources are successfully delivered. Moreover, the mission description allows for a fallback: if resources come from multiple storages, then multiple robots can be assigned to collect–deliver tasks in parallel.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Decompose G1 into Two Main Phases  
─────────────────────────────  
• G2 “Perform Collection Phase” – In this phase the robot navigates to the storage, sends a resource request, and waits for retrieval.  
• G3 “Perform Delivery Phase” – In this phase the robot navigates to the delivery location and makes the necessary runs to deliver all the resources.

Because the mission may also be executed via multi‐robot coordination when multiple storages are involved, we add an alternative goal:  
• G4 “Multi‐Robot Coordination” – This goal handles the assignment of multiple robots to perform collection and delivery in parallel.

The top‐level goal’s runtime annotation is modeled as a fallback between the sequential branch (G2 and G3) and the parallel branch (G4). In our notation, we use the operators “;” for sequential and “#” for parallel execution. Thus, G1 is annotated as:  

  Runtime: FALLBACK( ; , # )  

This indicates that if only a single storage is involved, the robot performs collection and delivery sequentially; if multiple storages are involved, the tasks are executed in parallel.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Decompose the Collection and Delivery Phases  
─────────────────────────────  

For the Collection Phase (G2):  
  • G2.1 “Navigate to Storage” (Perform)  
    – Target: The robot arrives at the designated storage.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
  • G2.2 “Request and Retrieve Resource” (Perform)  
    – Target: The robot sends the correct resource request and the resource is retrieved.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
  • G2.3 “Battery Check and Recharge in Collection” (Achieve)  
    – Target: If battery <10%, the robot returns to the recharging station and the mission is reassigned.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
    – Note: G2.3 is a fallback branch that is triggered only when the battery level is low.

For the Delivery Phase (G3):  
  • G3.1 “Navigate to Delivery Location” (Perform)  
    – Target: The robot arrives at the specified delivery location.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
  • G3.2 “Deliver Resource” (Perform)  
    – Target: The resource is delivered to the destination.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
  • G3.3 “Battery Check and Checkpoint Return in Delivery” (Achieve)  
    – Target: If battery <30%, the robot returns the resource to a checkpoint and reassigns the remaining task.  
    – Runtime: “-” (leaf)  
    – Note: This is again a fallback branch for battery management during delivery.

For Multi‐Robot Coordination (G4):  
  • G4 “Multi‐Robot Coordination” (Achieve)  
    – Target: All multi–robot tasks are coordinated successfully so that collection and delivery happen in parallel when resources are from different storages.  
    – Runtime: “#” (parallel)

─────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Associate Concrete Tasks with the Goal Decomposition  
─────────────────────────────  

For each leaf goal we assign one or more tasks. The tasks are concrete, actionable steps with a specified location and assigned number of robots. The assignments are as follows:

1. For G2.1 (“Navigate to Storage”):  
  Task AT1 “NavigateToStorage”:  
   – Location: Storage  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: The robot must navigate to the storage location.

2. For G2.2 (“Request and Retrieve Resource”):  
  Task AT2 “RequestRetrieveResource”:  
   – Location: Storage  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: The resource request and retrieval occur at the storage.

3. For G2.3 (“Battery Check and Recharge in Collection”):  
  Task AT3 “BatteryRechargeCollection”:  
   – Location: Recharging Station  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: Critical battery management during the collection phase.

4. For G3.1 (“Navigate to Delivery Location”):  
  Task AT4 “NavigateToDelivery”:  
   – Location: Specified Destination  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: Navigation to the delivery location is necessary.

5. For G3.2 (“Deliver Resource”):  
  Task AT5 “DeliverResource”:  
   – Location: Specified Destination  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: Final delivery step.

6. For G3.3 (“Battery Check and Checkpoint Return in Delivery”):  
  Task AT6 “BatteryCheckpointDelivery”:  
   – Location: Checkpoint  
   – Number of Robots: 1  
   – Justification: Ensures safe handling if battery is low during delivery.

7. For G4 (“Multi‐Robot Coordination”):  
  Task AT7 “AssignMultiRobotCollection”:  
   – Location: Multiple Storages  
   – Number of Robots: [1,n]  
   – Justification: Allows parallel collection tasks when resources are spread across different storages.  
  Task AT8 “AssignMultiRobotDelivery”:  
   – Location: Multiple Destinations  
   – Number of Robots: [1,n]  
   – Justification: Allows parallel delivery tasks to reduce overall mission time.

